※ pl4g-test: compile=ok opt=0,1 run=native,qemu exit=6 diag=none

※ An operator a module exports is in force here, and is written without naming the
※ module: there is no name to qualify an operator by, so putting it in force is the
※ only thing exporting one could mean.
※
※ This is what lets a library say what a glyph means for the types it deals in --
※ `⍕` for a number being the case it was found for.

let d := ⎕import("digits")

@[startup, impure]
fn main() → u6:
    let s: str = "n=" ⧺ ⍕1234u64
    ⎕narrow(⎕drop(#s), ⌜u6⌝) ?? 1u6
